  "content": "Owen's approach play is behind the Division I average on the two longer bands. His shortest band is the one exception.\n\n**Strokes gained approach:** There is no college figure for this metric, so I can't compare it to his level. He is at -4.16 strokes per round over his last 10 rounds, but only 8 of those rounds carry strokes gained data. That ranks 6th of 7 on the team, where the team figure is -3.09. The PGA Tour baseline is 0, so he is 4.16 strokes behind it.\n\n**Proximity (all recorded rounds):**\n- **50-125 yards:** He averages 23.77 ft from the hole. The Division I average is 28 ft, so he is 4.23 ft closer than the level. The Tour is 18 ft.\n- **125-175 yards:** He averages 50.58 ft. The Division I average is 38 ft, so he is 12.58 ft further away. The Tour is 30 ft.\n- **175+ yards:** He averages 94.65 ft. The Division I average is 55 ft, so he is 39.65 ft further away. The Tour is 45 ft.\n\nNo team proximity average could be read, so I can't place him against teammates on these bands.\n\n**Greens in regulation:** Over his last 10 rounds (144 holes) he hit 61.11%. The Division I average is 60%, and he is 5th of 7 on the team, whose figure is 62.73%. The Tour is 66%.\n\n**Takeaway:** Short approaches are a strength relative to the level. The loss comes from 125 yards and out, and it widens with distance. His GIR rate sits just above the level average, which fits with that.",
